WEBVTT

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:04.175
In every field, a float rod rises with the water.

00:00:04.615 --> 00:00:07.450
The more red rings we see, the higher the water.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.125
But leaves hide rings.

00:00:10.545 --> 00:00:14.050
A simple counter on this photo finds only eleven.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.150
So we don't count.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:23.875
We straighten the rod, find its top and the edge of the sleeve, and measure the striped part: nine hundred and one pixels.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:27.975
Down the rod, red and grey repeat like a heartbeat.

00:00:28.425 --> 00:00:32.050
Even where grass covers the rings, the rhythm doesn't change.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.250
Here, one ring every sixty-two pixels.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:39.475
Length divided by the gap: fourteen and a half rings.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:42.100
Hidden rings can't fool a measurement.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.000
The plain grey tip checks the scale.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:51.000
And when a photo is too hard, the system says so, and a person checks it, instead of guessing.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:52.900
Measure.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.575
Don't count.
